const proseCard = `app-card px-5 py-6 sm:px-8 sm:py-8 prose prose-base sm:prose-lg max-w-none
  prose-headings:font-extrabold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-headings:text-[color:var(--color-ink)]
  prose-headings:text-balance prose-headings:scroll-mt-24
  prose-h2:mt-12 prose-h2:mb-3 prose-h2:text-[1.5rem] sm:prose-h2:text-[1.75rem] prose-h2:leading-tight
  prose-h3:mt-8 prose-h3:mb-2
  prose-p:text-[color:var(--color-ink)] prose-p:leading-[1.75] prose-p:my-4
  prose-li:text-[color:var(--color-ink)] prose-li:leading-[1.7] prose-li:my-1.5
  prose-a:text-[color:var(--color-teal-dark)] prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline
  prose-strong:text-[color:var(--color-ink)]`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Fitbit Tracks Stress: Body Responses, HRV, EDA &amp; Stress Score Guide"
  description="How to track stress on Fitbit — what Body Responses, HRV, EDA scans and the Stress Management Score measure, what a good score looks like, and where Fitbit's stress tracking stops."
  image="/screenshots/wearable-insights.png"
  schema={schema}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-6 pb-16">
    <header class="px-1 mb-6">
      <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[color:var(--color-teal-dark)] font-semibold mb-2">Fitbit</div>
      <h1 class="text-[2rem] md:text-5xl leading-[1.1] font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-3 text-balance">Fitbit stress tracking, explained</h1>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] md:text-xl leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] text-pretty">
        Your Fitbit can't measure a stress hormone. Instead, it reads the body's signs of stress (heart rate, HRV, sweat response and sleep) and turns them into a daily rating. Here's how it works, where to find it, and where it stops.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] mt-4">
        Updated {new Date(UPDATED).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' })} · By <a href="/about/" class="underline underline-offset-2">Cortisol+ Editorial</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <InShort points={[
      'Fitbit tracks stress <strong>indirectly</strong>, from heart rate, HRV, skin sweat (EDA) and sleep. Not cortisol.',
      'Find it: Google Health app → <strong>Health</strong> → <strong>Mental wellbeing</strong> → <strong>Resilience</strong>.',
      'There\'s <strong>no "good" EDA score</strong>. Fewer EDA responses typically means calmer.',
    ]} />

    <!-- On this page -->
    <nav class="app-card overflow-hidden mb-6" aria-label="On this page">
      <div class="px-5 pt-4 pb-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-widest font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]">On this page</div>
      <ol class="app-list">
        {toc.map((item) => (
          <li><a href={`#${item.id}`} class="app-row text-[0.98rem] font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><span>{item.label}</span><span class="app-chevron" aria-hidden="true">›</span></a></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class={proseCard}>
      <p class="!text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] text-sm sm:text-base"><strong>2026 name changes:</strong> on May 19, 2026, the Fitbit app became the <strong>Google Health app</strong>. The Stress Management Score was renamed <strong>Resilience</strong>. It now shows Optimal, Balanced or Low instead of a number (<a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/17068213?hl=en">Google Health Help — what's new</a>). Your Fitbit device works the same way. Only the app labels moved.</p>

      <h2 id="body-responses">What are Body Responses on Fitbit?</h2>
      <p><strong>A Body Response is Fitbit's notice that your body is showing signs that could be stress.</strong></p>
      <p>On supported devices such as Fitbit Sense 2, a continuous electrodermal activity (cEDA) sensor watches for tiny changes in skin sweat. Fitbit combines these with heart rate, heart rate variability (HRV) and skin temperature. When those signals shift together, the app can notify you and ask you to log how you feel (<a href="https://blog.google/products/fitbit/how-we-trained-fitbits-body-response-feature-to-detect-stress/">Google's explanation of the feature</a>).</p>
      <p>Google's help page calls it a "sudden and rapid increase in autonomic nervous system activity". Body Responses don't track your overall stress. They only catch those sudden spikes.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Devices:</strong> the Fitbit Sense series and Google Pixel Watch 2 and later.</li>
        <li><strong>Timing:</strong> if notifications are on, the alert arrives about 10 minutes after the response (<a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en">Google Health Help — Manage stress &amp; mindfulness</a>).</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>What does "Body Responses" mean on Fitbit?</h3>
      <p>A Body Response means your nervous system reacted to something. That can be stress. It can also be exercise, excitement, heat or caffeine. That's why Fitbit asks you to label it. <strong>It is not a cortisol reading.</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Turn notifications on or off:</strong> in the Google Health app, tap Connections, then your device, then Notifications.</li>
        <li><strong>See past responses:</strong> go to Health → Mental wellbeing → Body responses, and switch between the day and week views.</li>
      </ul>

      <ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen/zen-coffee.webp">
        When a Body Response pops up, log what you were doing. <strong>Coffee, a workout or a hot room</strong> can trigger one too.
      </ZenTip>

      <h2 id="how-to-track-stress">How to track stress levels on Fitbit</h2>
      <p>Fitbit's stress features live in the Google Health app (the renamed Fitbit app). Step by step:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Wear a compatible device overnight and let it sync.</strong> Resilience works on Charge 4, 5 and 6, Inspire 2 and 3, Luxe, the Sense series, Versa 2, 3 and 4, Pixel Watch 2 and later, and Google Fitbit Air.</li>
        <li><strong>Open your daily Resilience rating:</strong> tap the <strong>Health</strong> tab. Under <em>Focus areas</em>, tap <strong>Mental wellbeing</strong> → <strong>Resilience</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Check Body Responses</strong> (Sense series, Pixel Watch 2 and later): from <strong>Mental wellbeing</strong>, tap <strong>Body responses</strong> for the day and week views. On the device, you can log your mood when an alert arrives.</li>
        <li><strong>Run an EDA scan</strong> (Charge 5, Charge 6, Sense): open the <strong>EDA Scan</strong> app on the device and stay still while you hold it. On Charge 5, hold the sides of the tracker with your thumb and index finger. The summary shows your number of EDA responses, and your heart rate at the start and end (<a href="https://www.fitbit.com/content/assets/help/manuals/manual_charge_5_en_US.pdf">Fitbit Charge 5 user manual</a>). These results now stay on the device.</li>
        <li><strong>Read the trend, not the day.</strong> Look at a week or two of ratings next to your <a href="/fitbit/hrv/">Fitbit HRV</a> and sleep before you draw conclusions.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Menus and device lists come from <a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en">Google Health Help</a>. They can differ by app version and region.</p>

      <h2 id="does-fitbit-track-stress">Does Fitbit track stress?</h2>
      <p><strong>Yes, but indirectly.</strong> Fitbit tracks the physical signs of stress. It doesn't track stress itself, or any stress hormone.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Every compatible device</strong> adds heart rate, HRV, exertion and sleep to the daily Resilience rating.</li>
        <li><strong>Devices with an EDA sensor</strong> add skin-sweat data.</li>
        <li><strong>Sense devices and Pixel Watch 2 and later</strong> also flag Body Responses in real time.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="how-fitbit-measures-stress">How does Fitbit measure stress?</h2>
      <p>Stress is a whole-body response run by your autonomic nervous system.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Under load</strong> (physical or mental), the "fight or flight" side (sympathetic) speeds up your heart, makes the beat-to-beat rhythm less variable, and makes your sweat glands more active.</li>
        <li><strong>When you recover</strong>, the "rest and digest" side (parasympathetic) takes over and reverses those changes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Fitbit can't see the hormones behind this. But it can see the body signals that follow, and that's what its stress features are built on.</p>
      <h3>The four signals Fitbit reads</h3>
      <p><strong>1. Heart rate variability (HRV).</strong> HRV is the change in time between one heartbeat and the next. Oddly, more variation is usually healthier. It reflects strong "rest and digest" (vagal) tone. Falling HRV is one of the most consistent body markers of stress load in the research. Fitbit measures HRV overnight, when things like caffeine and movement matter least. See <a href="/fitbit/hrv/">our full guide to Fitbit HRV</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>2. Resting and raised heart rate.</strong> A resting heart rate that runs higher than your normal, or stays high when you'd expect it to settle, is a classic sign that "fight or flight" is in charge. Fitbit tracks this all the time with the light-based (PPG) sensor on your wrist.</p>
      <p><strong>3. Electrodermal activity (EDA).</strong> Changes in skin sweat driven by "fight or flight". More on this below.</p>
      <p><strong>4. Sleep.</strong> Sleep both reflects stress and helps control it. Short or broken sleep raises how stirred up your body is the next day. Fitbit adds sleep quality and amount straight into its stress and readiness scores.</p>

      <ZenPicture src="/app-ui/start/zen_watch.webp" caption="Heart rate, HRV, skin sweat and sleep: the four signals behind Fitbit's stress features." />

      <h2 id="eda-sensor">What is the EDA sensor on Fitbit?</h2>
      <p>EDA stands for <strong>electrodermal activity</strong>. In Google's words, it's "tiny changes in the sweat level of your skin." Your "fight or flight" system controls your sweat glands. So these changes can show trends in how your body responds to stress (<a href="https://www.fitbit.com/content/assets/help/manuals/manual_charge_5_en_US.pdf">Fitbit Charge 5 manual</a>).</p>
      <p>On devices with an EDA sensor, such as Fitbit Sense and Charge 5 and later, you can run an EDA Scan by holding the device still with your hand or fingers. More responses during a scan can mean a more reactive stress state.</p>
      <p>Fitbit uses EDA in two ways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spot-check EDA scans</strong> run when you start them, on Charge 5, Charge 6 and Sense. Google says these results now stay on the device and no longer sync to the phone app.</li>
        <li><strong>Continuous EDA (cEDA)</strong> on the Sense series and Pixel Watch 2 and later runs in the background and powers Body Responses (<a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en">Google Health Help</a>).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="good-eda-score">What is a good EDA score on Fitbit?</h2>
      <p><strong>Fitbit doesn't define a "good" EDA score or a target number of responses.</strong></p>
      <p>Its only guidance is: "while your EDA response may vary, you should typically expect fewer EDA responses the calmer you are" (<a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en">Google Health Help</a>). Movement, noise and temperature changes can also trigger responses.</p>
      <p>How to use the number:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Scan at a similar time, in a similar place, sitting still.</li>
        <li>Compare with your own earlier scans.</li>
        <li>A lower count than usual suggests a calmer state. One high scan after coffee or a walk tells you little.</li>
      </ul>

      <ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_breathe.webp">
        Pick one calm, quiet time for your EDA scans, like before breakfast. <strong>Same time, same place</strong> makes them easier to compare.
      </ZenTip>

      <h2 id="resilience">Resilience: the old Stress Management Score</h2>
      <p>Each morning, compatible devices give you a daily rating of how well you can handle strain.</p>
      <p>Until May 2026, this was the <a href="/fitbit/stress-management-score/">Stress Management Score from 1 to 100</a>. <strong>It's now called Resilience, and shows Optimal, Balanced or Low.</strong></p>
      <p>Google says it's built from more than ten factors in three groups:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Responsiveness:</strong> heart rate, HRV and EDA data.</li>
        <li><strong>Exertion balance:</strong> your recent exercise compared with your longer-term activity.</li>
        <li><strong>Sleep patterns:</strong> how well and how much you sleep.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Resilience is not a Premium-only feature.</p>

      <h3>The Readiness Score</h3>
      <p>Fitbit's Readiness Score answers a different question: <strong>how ready is your body for exercise today?</strong></p>
      <p>It combines your <strong>HRV</strong>, <strong>resting heart rate</strong> and recent <strong>sleep</strong> into a 1–100 value:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Low:</strong> 29 or below</li>
        <li><strong>Moderate:</strong> 30–64</li>
        <li><strong>High:</strong> 65 or above</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A low readiness score after a stretch of poor sleep or high stress is a nudge to put recovery first, rather than a hard workout.</p>

      <a href="/start/" data-start-cta class="not-prose my-8 flex items-center gap-4 rounded-2xl bg-[color:var(--color-ink)] p-5 text-white no-underline shadow-sm transition hover:brightness-110">
        <img src="/apple-touch-icon.png" alt="" width="48" height="48" class="h-12 w-12 shrink-0 rounded-[13px]" />
        <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 leading-snug">
          <span class="block font-extrabold">Get your personal stress plan</span>
          <span class="block text-sm text-white/75">A one-minute check-in with Zen, then your plan in Cortisol+</span>
        </span>
        <span class="shrink-0 rounded-full bg-white px-4 py-2 text-sm font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Start ›</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="limits">What Fitbit stress tracking does — and doesn't — tell you</h2>
      <p><strong>What it does well:</strong> it gives you a measurable signal of how stirred up your body is, which you can watch as a trend over weeks. Falling HRV, a slowly rising resting heart rate and reactive EDA scans together paint a real picture of building strain.</p>
      <p><strong>What it can't do:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>measure cortisol or any hormone</li>
        <li>tell "good" stress from "bad" (a workout and an argument can look the same to the sensors)</li>
        <li>explain what caused a change</li>
      </ul>
      <p>It's a thermometer, not a diagnosis. <strong>Treat one low rating as noise, and a two-week trend as a signal.</strong></p>

      <h2>Where cortisol comes in</h2>
      <p>When most people say "stress", they mean the hormone cortisol. <strong>No consumer wearable, Fitbit included, measures it directly.</strong></p>
      <p>Wearables track <em>stand-ins</em> for cortisol's later effects, mainly HRV. We give the honest version in <a href="/fitbit/cortisol/">Can Fitbit track cortisol?</a></p>
      <p>Cortisol+ reads these signals from Apple Health on iPhone and Apple Watch, or from Health Connect on Android. Its score is a wellness estimate, not a cortisol test (<a href="/how-it-works/">see the methodology</a>). Cortisol+ is bringing its cortisol-pattern estimation to Fitbit data via Google Health Connect. That isn't a shipped feature yet.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14237928?hl=en" rel="nofollow">Google Health Help — Manage stress &amp; mindfulness (Resilience, Body responses, EDA)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/17068213?hl=en" rel="nofollow">Google Health Help — What is new with the redesigned Google Health app</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/google-health/google-health-app/" rel="nofollow">Google — Introducing the Google Health app</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://blog.google/products/fitbit/how-we-trained-fitbits-body-response-feature-to-detect-stress/" rel="nofollow">Google — How we trained Fitbit's Body Response feature to detect stress</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.fitbit.com/content/assets/help/manuals/manual_charge_5_en_US.pdf" rel="nofollow">Fitbit Charge 5 user manual — Use the EDA Scan app</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://support.google.com/fitbit/answer/14237928?hl=en" rel="nofollow">Fitbit / Google Health Help — Manage stress &amp; mindfulness (Stress Management Score)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://support.google.com/fitbit/answer/14236710?hl=en" rel="nofollow">Fitbit / Google Health Help — Readiness Score</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://support.google.com/fitbit/answer/14236917?hl=en" rel="nofollow">Fitbit / Google Health Help — Track your health metrics (HRV)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29486547/" rel="nofollow">Kim HG et al. Stress and Heart Rate Variability: A Meta-Analysis. Psychiatry Investig. 2018.</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5624990/" rel="nofollow">Shaffer F, Ginsberg JP. An Overview of Heart Rate Variability Metrics and Norms. Front Public Health. 2017.</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
      <FAQ items={faqs} />
    </div>
    <RelatedLinks links={related} />

    <aside class="mt-6 app-card p-5 text-sm text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Not medical advice.</strong> This page is educational. Fitbit, Google and Cortisol+ wearable scores are not hormone tests. For diagnosis, testing, or treatment of any medical condition, consult a qualified healthcare provider.
    </aside>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
